WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.604 --> 00:00:20.087
As we continued by train toward Dartmoor, Sherlock Holmes told me what the police had done so far.

2
00:00:20.107 --> 00:00:24.290
"Inspector Gregory is a very competent police officer," said Holmes.

3
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.874
"If only he had more imagination, he might rise to great heights in his profession.

4
00:00:29.694 --> 00:00:35.729
Upon arriving at Dartmoor, he did the obvious thing: he immediately found and arrested Fitzroy Simpson, the stranger

5
00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:36.809
who had visited the stable."

6
00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:39.030
"And who is Fitzroy Simpson?"

7
00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:39.790
I asked.

8
00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:44.031
"A well-educated gentleman who has squandered a fortune betting on horses.

9
00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:48.033
It's possible that he did not want Silver Blaze to win the Wessex Cup race."

10
00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:52.994
Holmes explained that Simpson had come to Dartmoor in hopes of getting more information on Silver Blaze.

11
00:00:54.474 --> 00:00:59.336
He was also interested in Desborough, the second-favorite horse in the race, who lived at the Mapleton stable.

12
00:01:00.232 --> 00:01:02.594
Simpson did not deny any of his actions on Monday night.

13
00:01:02.954 --> 00:01:07.257
But he said that he did not intend to hurt the horses; he only wanted information that would

14
00:01:07.297 --> 00:01:08.338
help him place his bets.

15
00:01:09.158 --> 00:01:11.940
"Did Simpson have any idea how the dead man came to be holding his scarf?"

16
00:01:12.881 --> 00:01:14.062
"No," said Holmes.

17
00:01:14.082 --> 00:01:17.444
"When confronted with that evidence, he turned very pale."

18
00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:21.547
Simpson's wet clothing showed that he had been out in the rain the night before.

19
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:26.090
And his walking stick was just the sort of weapon that could have killed Straker, the trainer.

20
00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:31.245
"You have laid out all the facts," I said, "but I still don't understand what the police theory

21
00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:34.908
is." "The police think that Fitzroy Simpson drugged the groom.

22
00:01:36.449 --> 00:01:41.253
Then he found a duplicate key to the stable, unlocked the door, and stole the horse," explained Holmes.

23
00:01:41.273 --> 00:01:45.756
"While Simpson was leading the horse across the moor, he was overtaken by the trainer.

24
00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:50.119
Naturally, they fought, and Simpson beat Straker to death with his walking stick.

25
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.483
Then Simpson either led the horse to some secret hiding place or the horse bolted during the struggle

26
00:01:55.563 --> 00:01:56.744
and is now wandering the moor.

27
00:01:57.652 --> 00:02:00.934
It's an improbable theory, but I can't test it until we are on the spot."

28
00:02:01.815 --> 00:02:05.638
It was late afternoon before we reached the town of Tavistock, which lay in the center of the

29
00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:06.198
great moor.

30
00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:11.782
Two men were waiting for us at the station: Inspector Gregory, a tall man carrying a bag, and

31
00:02:11.842 --> 00:02:13.483
Colonel Ross, a small, dapper man.

32
00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:15.764
"Have there been any new developments?"

33
00:02:16.185 --> 00:02:16.725
asked Holmes.

34
00:02:16.745 --> 00:02:21.348
"I'm sorry to say that we've made very little progress," said Inspector Gregory.

35
00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:26.532
A minute later we were seated in a carriage, and Gregory began to talk about the case.

36
00:02:27.612 --> 00:02:30.394
"I still believe that Simpson is our man," the inspector said.

37
00:02:30.414 --> 00:02:33.396
"The evidence against him is certainly very strong."

38
00:02:34.297 --> 00:02:35.138
Holmes shook his head.

39
00:02:35.158 --> 00:02:38.380
"A clever lawyer would tear your case to shreds.

40
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.703
There was no need for Simpson to take the horse from the stable if he wished to harm

41
00:02:42.723 --> 00:02:45.825
it. Where is the paper he wanted the maid to give to the groom?

42
00:02:46.726 --> 00:02:47.867
Where is the duplicate key?

43
00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:50.528
Who sold him the powdered opium?

44
00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:55.192
Where could he—a stranger to the moor—have found a place to hide the horse?"

45
00:02:56.056 --> 00:02:58.578
But Gregory had an answer for all of Holmes' questions.

46
00:02:59.498 --> 00:03:02.440
The paper was a ten-pound note and it was found in Simpson's pocket.

47
00:03:03.301 --> 00:03:04.842
Simpson could have tossed away the key.

48
00:03:06.263 --> 00:03:07.864
He could have brought the opium from London.

49
00:03:08.765 --> 00:03:11.927
He was not a stranger, having lodged twice in the nearby guesthouses.

50
00:03:12.747 --> 00:03:16.190
The horse could be at the bottom of one of the pits or old mines upon the moor.

51
00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.933
Simpson might also have led the horse to some gypsies, who were camped near the murder site.

52
00:03:20.953 --> 00:03:25.056
"I understand that there is another training stable nearby," said Holmes.

53
00:03:25.928 --> 00:03:30.491
"Since the Mapleton horse was the second favorite, that stable certainly had an interest in seeing that Silver

54
00:03:30.531 --> 00:03:32.792
Blaze couldn't run the race," said Gregory.

55
00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:36.354
"And the trainer, Silas Brown, was no friend of Straker's.

56
00:03:37.194 --> 00:03:40.556
But we have examined the stable and there is nothing to connect Brown to the affair."

57
00:03:40.576 --> 00:03:44.038
"And nothing to link Simpson with the Mapleton stable?"

58
00:03:44.398 --> 00:03:44.918
asked Holmes.

59
00:03:44.938 --> 00:03:47.720
"Nothing at all," replied Gregory.

60
00:03:49.201 --> 00:03:52.222
By this time we had reached the red-brick house where Straker had lived.

61
00:03:53.043 --> 00:03:54.924
We all sprang from the carriage except for Holmes.

62
00:03:55.892 --> 00:03:59.874
It was only when I touched his arm that he roused himself with a violent start and stepped

63
00:03:59.894 --> 00:04:00.514
from the carriage.

64
00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:02.975
Colonel Ross looked at him with surprise.

65
00:04:02.995 --> 00:04:05.456
"Excuse me," said Holmes.

66
00:04:05.476 --> 00:04:07.377
"I must have been daydreaming."

67
00:04:08.177 --> 00:04:10.118
But I could tell that he was excited about something.

68
00:04:10.958 --> 00:04:13.539
He must have found a clue although I had no idea what it was.

69
00:04:14.479 --> 00:04:17.261
Inside the house Holmes asked Ross about Straker's service.

70
00:04:17.281 --> 00:04:21.022
"I always found him to be an excellent servant," the colonel replied.

71
00:04:21.822 --> 00:04:25.244
Next Holmes inquired about the items that had been in the trainer's pockets at the time of his

72
00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:31.287
death. These included matches, a two-inch candle, a pipe and tobacco, and a few papers.

73
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.729
Plus, he'd been holding a small knife with a delicate blade.

74
00:04:34.749 --> 00:04:38.631
"This is an unusual knife," said Holmes, examining it.

75
00:04:38.651 --> 00:04:41.713
"Isn't this something you'd use in your profession, Watson?"

76
00:04:41.733 --> 00:04:43.754
"Yes," I answered.

77
00:04:43.774 --> 00:04:46.355
"It's used for the most delicate eye surgery."

78
00:04:46.375 --> 00:04:49.397
"A strange thing for a horse trainer to carry with him.

79
00:04:50.278 --> 00:04:53.740
It's too small to be of much use for self-defense," said Holmes.

80
00:04:54.716 --> 00:04:58.959
"Straker's wife said he picked it up from his dressing table as he left the house," said Gregory.

81
00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:02.462
"It was a poor weapon, but maybe the best he could find at that moment."

82
00:05:03.322 --> 00:05:04.824
"Possibly," said Holmes.

83
00:05:04.844 --> 00:05:06.525
"What are these papers?"

84
00:05:07.385 --> 00:05:12.269
They turned out to be bills from a hay dealer, some instructions from Colonel Ross, and a dressmaker's

85
00:05:12.309 --> 00:05:14.290
bill addressed to William Derbyshire.

86
00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:20.295
"Mrs. Straker told us that Derbyshire was a friend of her husband's and that his mail sometimes came

87
00:05:20.355 --> 00:05:21.696
here," explained Gregory.

88
00:05:22.684 --> 00:05:26.706
"Mrs. Derbyshire has expensive taste," said Holmes, glancing at the bill.

89
00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:29.387
"She paid quite a lot for just one dress."

90
00:05:30.347 --> 00:05:31.908
As we emerged from the sitting room, Mrs.

91
00:05:31.988 --> 00:05:32.628
Straker came.

92
00:05:32.648 --> 00:05:35.149
"Have you found out who killed my husband?"

93
00:05:35.489 --> 00:05:35.990
she cried.

94
00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.811
"Not yet, but Sherlock Holmes has come to help us."

95
00:05:39.791 --> 00:05:41.072
"Surely we've met before, Mrs.

96
00:05:41.112 --> 00:05:42.392
Straker," said Holmes.

97
00:05:42.412 --> 00:05:47.635
"It was at a garden party and you were wearing a gray silk dress trimmed with ostrich feathers."

98
00:05:48.615 --> 00:05:49.175
"No, sir."

99
00:05:49.595 --> 00:05:50.716
Mrs. Straker looked puzzled.

100
00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.781
"I've never owned such a dress."

101
00:05:53.682 --> 00:05:57.184
And with an apology Holmes followed the inspector and Colonel Ross outside.

102
00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:01.247
We then moved on to the spot where Straker's body was found.

103
00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:07.851
"In this bag," said Gregory, "I have a boot that Straker was wearing, one of Simpson's shoes, and

104
00:06:07.891 --> 00:06:09.572
a horseshoe belonging to Silver Blaze."

105
00:06:10.513 --> 00:06:14.816
Gregory handed the bag over to Holmes, who began a careful examination of the muddy ground.

106
00:06:15.756 --> 00:06:18.238
Suddenly he picked up what looked like a little chip of wood.

107
00:06:18.258 --> 00:06:20.219
"A burned match!"

108
00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:21.180
he exclaimed.

109
00:06:22.056 --> 00:06:23.197
"I thought we might find this."

110
00:06:24.158 --> 00:06:27.280
Gregory studied the match, but Colonel Ross had other things on his mind.

111
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.242
"I'm thinking I should remove Silver Blaze's name from the race," he said.

112
00:06:31.262 --> 00:06:32.964
"Certainly not!"

113
00:06:33.364 --> 00:06:33.924
cried Holmes.

114
00:06:33.944 --> 00:06:35.865
"It's too soon for that."

115
00:06:36.746 --> 00:06:38.007
Colonel Ross looked at Holmes.

116
00:06:38.027 --> 00:06:44.611
"Well," said Silver Blaze's owner with a sneer, "I suppose a London detective knows more about these things

117
00:06:44.651 --> 00:06:45.192
than I do."
